WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.868 --> 00:00:01.968
-Our first guest tonight

00:00:01.968 --> 00:00:03.400
is an Emmy- and
Tony Award-winning actor

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.167
you know from movies like
"The Birdcage"

00:00:05.167 --> 00:00:07.968
and "The Lion King," as well as
shows like "The Gilded Age"

00:00:07.968 --> 00:00:10.000
and "Only Murders
in the Building."

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:11.868
He's currently starring
in "Pictures from Home"

00:00:11.868 --> 00:00:13.200
on Broadway's Studio 54.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:14.834
And you can see him
in "Beau Is Afraid,"

00:00:14.834 --> 00:00:16.667
which is in theaters now.
Let's take a look.

00:00:16.667 --> 00:00:18.834
-What is the room is?

00:00:18.834 --> 00:00:21.234
-This is a room in our house,

00:00:21.234 --> 00:00:24.133
but it's your home
for as long as you need.

00:00:24.133 --> 00:00:26.367
My name's Grace.
[ Knock on door ]

00:00:26.367 --> 00:00:29.300
Oh, this is Roger.
This is my husband.

00:00:33.834 --> 00:00:36.834
-Hey, guy.
Welcome back.

00:00:36.834 --> 00:00:38.868
Thought you'd sleep in, huh?

00:00:38.868 --> 00:00:41.400
-Roger's a very
respected surgeon.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:44.434
He's the one who dressed
and treated your wounds.

00:00:44.434 --> 00:00:45.400
-You're a lucky man.

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:48.968
[ Device beeps ]

00:00:48.968 --> 00:00:51.367
-What is --
what is this?

00:00:51.367 --> 00:00:53.634
-That's my little
assistant health monitor.

00:00:53.634 --> 00:00:55.634
Keeps track of your condition.

00:00:55.634 --> 00:00:56.934
-Please welcome back
to the show

00:00:56.934 --> 00:00:59.701
our good friend Nathan Lane,
everybody!

00:00:59.701 --> 00:01:01.601
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:01.601 --> 00:01:11.601
♪♪

00:01:11.601 --> 00:01:21.601
♪♪

00:01:21.601 --> 00:01:24.167
Look at you!
Look at how fit you are!

00:01:24.167 --> 00:01:26.834
-Oh, jogging over here,
I saw Tucker Carlson

00:01:26.834 --> 00:01:29.567
in a bar chugging Bud Light.
He's upset.

00:01:29.567 --> 00:01:30.767
[ Laughter ]
-Oh, no!

00:01:30.767 --> 00:01:32.901
-He is so upset.
-He is upset.

00:01:32.901 --> 00:01:34.033
-Oh, Seth, I --

00:01:34.033 --> 00:01:35.133
-It breaks your heart,
doesn't it?

00:01:35.133 --> 00:01:37.467
-Yeah. Eeee!
[ Laughter ]

00:01:37.467 --> 00:01:40.234
You know, I see you backstage.
-Yeah.

00:01:40.234 --> 00:01:43.467
-I thought we were doing
a podcast, but then it hit me.

00:01:43.467 --> 00:01:46.701
This is just your "Every day
is Casual Friday" look.

00:01:46.701 --> 00:01:48.167
-Yeah.
-And I have to --

00:01:48.167 --> 00:01:51.100
I have to admit
that it has inspired me.

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:54.434
I mean, look at you. You --
You are a rhapsody in J.Crew.

00:01:54.434 --> 00:01:56.434
[ Laughter ]

00:01:56.434 --> 00:01:59.334
I love that you brought
normcore fashion to late night,

00:01:59.334 --> 00:02:02.767
and with this look,
you have totally embraced

00:02:02.767 --> 00:02:05.834
the role
of America's late-night zaddy!

00:02:05.834 --> 00:02:07.300
-Oh, well, thank you.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:09.634
Thank you so much.

00:02:09.634 --> 00:02:11.434
-You know, I --

00:02:11.434 --> 00:02:13.767
I used to dress
like I was interviewing

00:02:13.767 --> 00:02:15.534
with the C.E.O.
of a tech start-up,

00:02:15.534 --> 00:02:19.067
but then you, you --
you showed me the way.

00:02:19.067 --> 00:02:21.701
You showed me that I was
just trying too hard

00:02:21.701 --> 00:02:25.167
and it was time
to finally give up

00:02:25.167 --> 00:02:29.000
and embrace the joy
of the tracksuit.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.567
-Yeah.
-I love tracksuits.

00:02:31.567 --> 00:02:33.501
-It's lovely.
-Tracksuit.

00:02:33.501 --> 00:02:35.200
It's fashion's way of saying,

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:37.267
"I have no more [bleep]
to give."

00:02:37.267 --> 00:02:39.801
[ Laughter ]

00:02:39.801 --> 00:02:43.033
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:43.033 --> 00:02:46.400
Right? You know,
now I can look athletic

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:49.334
and I don't have
to do a damn thing.

00:02:49.334 --> 00:02:52.267
I always look like
I'm about to go for a run,

00:02:52.267 --> 00:02:54.200
but something
more important came up.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:55.534
[ Laughter ]

00:02:55.534 --> 00:02:56.567
You know, the last time --

00:02:56.567 --> 00:02:58.033
I was supposed to be here
a while ago.

00:02:58.033 --> 00:02:59.667
-Yeah, but I had COVID.
We had to --

00:02:59.667 --> 00:03:01.667
-Now, do you believe
it's real?

00:03:01.667 --> 00:03:04.434
[ Laughter ]

00:03:04.434 --> 00:03:09.033
And the time --
the time before that,

00:03:09.033 --> 00:03:10.334
it was the height
of the pandemic.

00:03:10.334 --> 00:03:11.367
There was no audience.

00:03:11.367 --> 00:03:13.100
It was like sailing
on a ghost ship,

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:15.234
just me desperately
trying to make you

00:03:15.234 --> 00:03:16.501
and three cameramen laugh.

00:03:16.501 --> 00:03:18.834
-Yeah.
-So -- But here we are.

00:03:18.834 --> 00:03:20.801
-And here we are with a really,
really nice audience.

00:03:20.801 --> 00:03:22.567
-Really nice.
-It's pretty nice.

00:03:22.567 --> 00:03:24.868
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:24.868 --> 00:03:28.734
-We are -- We are --
We're reunited showbiz brothers

00:03:28.734 --> 00:03:31.434
like Woody Harrelson
and Matthew McConaughey,

00:03:31.434 --> 00:03:34.834
the greatest "Maury Povich"
episode that never was.

00:03:34.834 --> 00:03:36.300
[ Laughter ]
Yes.

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:40.100
-You -- I want to compliment you
on something, which is,

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:43.601
since the last time I saw you,
you won an Emmy, sir,

00:03:43.601 --> 00:03:46.901
for your work in "Only..."
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:46.901 --> 00:03:49.734
The wonderful show
"Only Murders in the Building,"

00:03:49.734 --> 00:03:51.767
which just a tremendous cast

00:03:51.767 --> 00:03:53.200
and one of the best things
in the world,

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:55.100
which is a television show
that got made in New York City.

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:57.567
-I know. And with Marty Short
and Steve Martin

00:03:57.567 --> 00:03:59.267
and Selena Gomez.
-Yeah, that's a --

00:03:59.267 --> 00:04:02.133
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:02.133 --> 00:04:05.300
-Yes, it was --
It was my seventh nomination.

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:06.801
It was nice to finally win,

00:04:06.801 --> 00:04:09.467
although it was
a silent episode.

00:04:09.467 --> 00:04:12.267
So all these years, the Academy

00:04:12.267 --> 00:04:14.234
was just waiting for me
to shut up.

00:04:14.234 --> 00:04:15.634
[ Laughter ]

00:04:15.634 --> 00:04:18.634
-You -- This is
the final week of a show.

00:04:18.634 --> 00:04:20.167
-That's right.
-You've done a lot of --

00:04:20.167 --> 00:04:22.300
-Beautiful play.
-And you've done many

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:24.467
over the years.
How are you with endings?

00:04:24.467 --> 00:04:26.601
Do you get a little bittersweet

00:04:26.601 --> 00:04:28.334
when you wrap up
a show like this?

00:04:28.334 --> 00:04:31.434
-Well, this one in particular.
I have loved doing this play.

00:04:31.434 --> 00:04:33.133
It's a beautiful play
by Sharr White,

00:04:33.133 --> 00:04:35.467
directed by Bart Sher,
and starring the wonderful

00:04:35.467 --> 00:04:37.901
Danny Burstein
and Zoe Wanamaker.

00:04:37.901 --> 00:04:39.467
And this is --
Yes, this is our last week.

00:04:39.467 --> 00:04:42.767
So if you're in town,
come to Studio 54 Theatre.

00:04:42.767 --> 00:04:44.734
And, really, because
I've been thinking,

00:04:44.734 --> 00:04:47.133
this is probably
my theatrical swan song,

00:04:47.133 --> 00:04:48.434
my last Broadway show.

00:04:48.434 --> 00:04:50.534
-Now, that -- that is very --
[ Audience booing ]

00:04:50.534 --> 00:04:51.667
That's problematic.

00:04:51.667 --> 00:04:53.667
-Well, you see, Seth,

00:04:53.667 --> 00:04:55.801
things have gotten crazy
on the Broadway now.

00:04:55.801 --> 00:04:57.467
I'm sure you've read about it.

00:04:57.467 --> 00:04:58.968
Audiences are out of control.

00:04:58.968 --> 00:05:00.367
People are pooping
in the audience.

00:05:00.367 --> 00:05:01.868
-No.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:01.868 --> 00:05:03.400
-They're heckling the actors.

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:07.300
There's fistfights, dog fights,
deliveries from DoorDash.

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:10.601
-No! Mid-show.
DoorDash will show up mid-show?

00:05:10.601 --> 00:05:13.234
-Beer pong.
People are having sex,

00:05:13.234 --> 00:05:14.868
not even waiting
until intermission.

00:05:14.868 --> 00:05:17.000
It's a nightmare.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:19.200
-That's very hard.
As an actor, that's very hard.

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:20.767
-Well, the pooping is the worst.
-Yeah.

00:05:20.767 --> 00:05:22.067
[ Laughter ]

00:05:22.067 --> 00:05:25.400
And because it's happening
with alarming frequency

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:28.334
on any given night
in a Broadway theater,

00:05:28.334 --> 00:05:30.534
the 11:00 number is two.

00:05:30.534 --> 00:05:33.467
[ Laughter ]

00:05:33.467 --> 00:05:36.067
No, if you're gonna --
if you're gonna crap

00:05:36.067 --> 00:05:39.067
in the aisle, save it
for a Southwest flight.

00:05:39.067 --> 00:05:40.701
[ Laughter ]

00:05:40.701 --> 00:05:44.667
Anyway, I was reminded
of our conversation,

00:05:44.667 --> 00:05:47.234
and I think I may have
one more show.

00:05:47.234 --> 00:05:49.567
-I'm so glad you said that,
because we have talked about --

00:05:49.567 --> 00:05:52.000
You know, we have
this collaboration in the works.

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:53.834
-"Cicada, Cicada."
-"Cicada, Cicada."

00:05:53.834 --> 00:05:55.234
-Just say it.
-Just say it.

00:05:55.234 --> 00:05:56.467
-I mean, the audience
wants to hear.

00:05:56.467 --> 00:06:00.434
"Cicada, Cicada" is a show.
And we might --

00:06:00.434 --> 00:06:02.801
We might be closer
to getting it on its feet.

00:06:02.801 --> 00:06:05.567
Do you want to remind people
of the plot as you remember it?

00:06:05.567 --> 00:06:07.367
-Sure. Yes.
The basic plot line is,

00:06:07.367 --> 00:06:09.000
our dear friend,
Christine Baranski,

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:11.000
would play a cicada
who comes to New York,

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:13.567
not realizing
she only has 17 days,

00:06:13.567 --> 00:06:18.100
the life span of a cicada,
to live her best Big Apple life.

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:22.100
She's befriended by a very
gregarious cockroach,

00:06:22.100 --> 00:06:24.434
played by the great
Harvey Fierstein,

00:06:24.434 --> 00:06:25.834
in Times Square.

00:06:25.834 --> 00:06:29.534
And he actually resides
in the glamorous apartment

00:06:29.534 --> 00:06:31.434
of a big Broadway star,
played by me.

00:06:31.434 --> 00:06:33.968
-There you go.
-And I've come to terms

00:06:33.968 --> 00:06:36.300
with the fact
that this is a version of me

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:38.367
with a cockroach-infested
apartment.

00:06:38.367 --> 00:06:39.934
[ Laughter ]

00:06:39.934 --> 00:06:43.434
I don't -- I don't like it,
but I've come to terms with it.

00:06:43.434 --> 00:06:46.167
And a lot of the New York Mets

00:06:46.167 --> 00:06:47.701
will be playing
supporting roles.

00:06:47.701 --> 00:06:50.434
-Yeah.
-Now, I think first baseman

00:06:50.434 --> 00:06:54.100
Pete Alonso would be an inspired
choice for the antagonist.

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:56.067
-Yeah.
-Perhaps the exterminator.

00:06:56.067 --> 00:06:58.501
-Oh, very good.
-And we have the tagline,

00:06:58.501 --> 00:07:01.234
"Cicada, Cicada" --
The whole town is abuzz.

00:07:01.234 --> 00:07:02.667
So...
-There you go.

00:07:02.667 --> 00:07:04.601
-...aside from not having
a script,

00:07:04.601 --> 00:07:07.267
I think we're 90% there.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:07.267 --> 00:07:10.000
Now, the big news is
that I reached out

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:11.801
to two of my oldest
and dearest friends,

00:07:11.801 --> 00:07:13.133
Marc Shaiman
and Scott Wittman,

00:07:13.133 --> 00:07:15.467
the Tony-winning geniuses
behind "Hairspray"...

00:07:15.467 --> 00:07:17.234
-Geniuses.
-And their current smash hit

00:07:17.234 --> 00:07:18.968
"Some Like It Hot,"
and I asked them

00:07:18.968 --> 00:07:22.167
if they would write the score,
and they said, yes, they're in!

00:07:22.167 --> 00:07:23.701
-So...
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:23.701 --> 00:07:27.200
-So...
[ Laughs ]

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:28.501
So the next time I'm here --

00:07:28.501 --> 00:07:30.334
I have another movie
coming out this summer.

00:07:30.334 --> 00:07:34.968
The next time I'm here,
so it'll be very soon,

00:07:34.968 --> 00:07:37.133
it will be --
if we work out the billing

00:07:37.133 --> 00:07:41.100
and all the deals go through,
it'll be me, Christine Baranski,

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:43.000
Harvey Fierstein,
and the New York Mets

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:47.267
in the act-one finale of
"Cicada, Cicada."

00:07:47.267 --> 00:07:50.234
What I'm saying is,
start sewing the costumes now.

00:07:50.234 --> 00:07:53.367
-I am so excited.
Your enthusiasm for this is --

00:07:53.367 --> 00:07:55.167
It's incredible to me.
-I can't wait.

00:07:55.167 --> 00:07:56.567
-You mentioned you have a movie
coming out this summer.

00:07:56.567 --> 00:07:58.534
You have this --
You know, we showed a clip

00:07:58.534 --> 00:08:01.667
of this movie, Joaquin Phoenix,
the great Amy Ryan, another

00:08:01.667 --> 00:08:04.000
"Only Murders in the Building"
alum right there.

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:05.400
-Yes.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:09.367
-And this director has done
some of the trippiest movies

00:08:09.367 --> 00:08:11.234
in recent years.
He did "Midsommar."

00:08:11.234 --> 00:08:13.234
He did the fantastic film
"Hereditary."

00:08:13.234 --> 00:08:15.033
-Yes.
This is his third film.

00:08:15.033 --> 00:08:17.634
-This is his third film.
This is very cool.

00:08:17.634 --> 00:08:21.467
And, I mean, a sort of
out-there plot, would you say?

00:08:21.467 --> 00:08:23.601
-Yes.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:23.601 --> 00:08:25.067
I would say it's --

00:08:25.067 --> 00:08:27.701
You know, look, it's --

00:08:27.701 --> 00:08:29.267
I'll be honest, it's --

00:08:29.267 --> 00:08:31.100
I think it's
an extraordinary film.

00:08:31.100 --> 00:08:35.801
And I think he's -- he's
an astonishing young director.

00:08:35.801 --> 00:08:38.167
But it's been
a divisive film.

00:08:38.167 --> 00:08:40.000
You know, it's --

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:41.434
There's been a lot of chatter

00:08:41.434 --> 00:08:43.567
about the fact
that it's three hours.

00:08:43.567 --> 00:08:46.467
And as I like to say,
it's not a prostate exam.

00:08:46.467 --> 00:08:48.033
It's --
[ Laughter ]

00:08:48.033 --> 00:08:50.834
It's the --
[ Laughter ]

00:08:50.834 --> 00:08:52.801
You know, it's a unique

00:08:52.801 --> 00:08:55.934
and challenging
cinematic experience.

00:08:55.934 --> 00:08:58.701
And for those who want that,
there it is.

00:08:58.701 --> 00:09:00.300
-And there's less
of that every year.

00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:02.334
-Oh, I know.
-So I embrace that.

00:09:02.334 --> 00:09:04.701
-Because I mean, you know,
a $15 ticket

00:09:04.701 --> 00:09:07.901
to see Joaquin Phoenix emote for
80 minutes would be a bargain.

00:09:07.901 --> 00:09:10.567
To see him do it for 100 minutes
would be an even better deal.

00:09:10.567 --> 00:09:13.334
But what if I told you, Seth,
you got Joaquin Phoenix,

00:09:13.334 --> 00:09:15.100
the greatest actor
of his generation,

00:09:15.100 --> 00:09:17.367
for 80 minutes
and then 100 more minutes

00:09:17.367 --> 00:09:18.901
for the same low price?
[ Laughter ]

00:09:18.901 --> 00:09:20.367
-That's fantastic.

00:09:20.367 --> 00:09:23.834
-And at no additional charge,
you get Amy Ryan,

00:09:23.834 --> 00:09:25.934
yours truly,
Parker Posey, Patti LuPone,

00:09:25.934 --> 00:09:27.734
and I'll even throw in
Richard Kind.

00:09:27.734 --> 00:09:30.234
-Richard Kind?!
-Richard Kind!

00:09:30.234 --> 00:09:31.667
And a Perfect Pasta Pot.

00:09:31.667 --> 00:09:34.868
You'd probably say
it's too good to be true.

00:09:34.868 --> 00:09:36.901
And you'd be right.
There's no Pasta Pot...

00:09:36.901 --> 00:09:38.267
[ Laughter ]

00:09:38.267 --> 00:09:41.100
...but there is Richard Kind.
-Richard Kind.

00:09:41.100 --> 00:09:42.868
-And so
it's a pretty sweet deal.

00:09:42.868 --> 00:09:44.601
What are you people
waiting for?!

00:09:44.601 --> 00:09:46.734
[ Laughter, cheers, applause ]

00:09:46.734 --> 00:09:51.033
-One more time for my friend
Nathan Lane, everybody!

00:09:51.033 --> 00:09:53.801
"Pictures from Home"
is on Broadway at Studio 54.

00:09:53.801 --> 00:09:55.601
"Beau is Afraid"
is in theaters now.

00:09:55.601 --> 00:09:57.834
We'll be right back
with Congresswoman Katie Porter.

